INT. IRONWORKS GYM - EARLY MORNING

Fluorescent tubes buzz awake one bank at a time. Rubber mats. Chalk dust drifting in the light. A treadmill whirs somewhere in the back.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every gym smells the same. Sweat, bleach, and people trying to outrun something.

Jess, 30s, a former detective turned personal trainer, wipes down a bench. Her knuckles are scabbed. She does not look like she sleeps.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
That's me. Jess. I used to chase the wrong people for a living. Now I help them chase their goals.

The front door CHIMES. A man in a grey hoodie hovers by the desk, hood up, hands deep in his pockets.

JESS
We're closed till six.

HOODIE MAN
I'm not here to train.

Quiet.

JESS
Then why?

HOODIE MAN
Someone said you used to work cases.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Trouble always walks in with its hood up.

Jess sets down the towel. Slowly.

JESS
Used to.

He pulls out a photograph and slides it across the desk. A young woman, smiling, in a gym tank top.

HOODIE MAN
That's my sister. She trained here. Two weeks ago she stopped answering.

JESS
Lots of people quit. Gyms are full of people who quit.

HOODIE MAN
Not her.

Silence.

Jess studies the photo. Then the man. Then the photo again.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The photo had a date on the back. A date she shouldn't have known.

JESS
What's your name?

HOODIE MAN
Does it matter?

JESS
It does to me.

He hesitates. Pulls the hood down. Mid-thirties. Tired eyes.

HOODIE MAN (CONT'D)
Kyle.

KYLE
Kyle Brandt.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Brandt. I knew that name. I had buried that name.

Jess goes still. The treadmill in the back keeps whirring, steady, patient.

JESS
Kyle. How long have you known where I work?

KYLE
Since the funeral.

Nothing.

A locker door CLANGS in the back. Both turn.

From behind the lockers steps Big Mo, 50s, the gym owner, wiping his hands on a towel. He is too casual.

BIG MO
Everything okay out here?

KYLE
Fine.

JESS
Fine.

BIG MO
Because it sounds like a conversation I should be in.

Nobody answers. The towel in his hand is red at the corner.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mo kept a clean gym. Always. Except that morning.

Kyle's eyes drop to the towel. Then to Jess.

KYLE
(quiet)
He's the last person who saw her.

BIG MO
Kid, you don't want to say that.

KYLE
I just did.

Silence.

Jess steps between them without seeming to. Her hand finds the heavy kettlebell on the shelf.

JESS
Mo. Open the back room.

BIG MO
It's just storage.

JESS
Then it won't take long.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That was the last honest thing any of us said in that building.

Mo smiles. It is not a happy smile. He drops the towel.

Behind the lockers, a phone begins to RING. Soft. Persistent.

All three look toward the sound.

The ringtone is a pop song the sister used to hum while she lifted.
